\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Ctrl Bk Chief Expects Low Inflation, Good Growth  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eduardo Kaplan and Robert Kozak  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 990 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2005\par 18:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Any election-related volatility next year shouldn't derail what has so far been a four-year streak of record low inflation and robust economic growth for Peru, according to the nation's top monetary official.  \par \par \uc2 During a wide-ranging interview with Dow Jones Newswires late Tuesday, the Central Reserve Bank of Peru's acting President, Oscar Dancourt, underlined the bank's commitment to its inflation targeting model, saying that keeping prices under control remains the key mission of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Describing a benign international environment in which Peru's main export buyers - the United States and China - are expected to grow at current levels and in which commodity prices are expected to remain strong, Dancourt said the economy should expand by 5.0% next year, slightly below this year's 5.5% official forecast.  \par \par \uc2 He said that even with high prices for crude oil, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  budgets at $65 per barrel next year, fiscal, economic and monetary stability should persist well into next year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also forecasts exports reaching $16.1 billion this year and rising to $16.9 billion next year, underscoring the boom in international sales of goods such as minerals, textiles and fish meal.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't see any negative effects from the election," Dancourt said in the interview in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's art-filled headquarters in Lima's historic center.  \par \par \uc2 Peru faces general elections early next year and both a new president and a new Congress will take over in July. President Alejandro Toledo can't run for a second term in office in 2006 due to term limits.  \par \par \uc2 Credit rating agencies have said that any upgrade in Peru's below investment grade rating depends largely on a smooth transition of power.  \par \par \uc2 Before the campaign starts, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had its hands full dealing with a fast appreciating local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, as well as helping to develop a sol-denominated yield curve.  \par \par \uc2 In the last few years, Peru has increased its issuance of sol-denominated debt in the local market, drawing foreign investors with attractive yields in longer-dated maturities.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt said keeping inflation under control is the best way to dampen any possible election-related market concerns among foreign buyers of the sol-denominated debt, some of which Peru placed recently to repay obligations to the Paris Club of private creditors.  \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley said in a market report this week that international investors now hold around three quarters of the longer-term issues.  \par \par \uc2 "On the back of the substantial rally in local rates, as well as the unexpected bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, there is concern that international investors could take profits," the investment bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The sol appreciated by 5.5% against the dollar last year, but Dancourt noted that it remains essentially a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The best approach to allay concerns about its strength, he said, is to keep inflation low.  \par \par \uc2 However, he also indicated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to maintain its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if volatility becomes excessive, pointing to \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong international reserves. Reserves totaled $13.70 billion as of Sept. 20.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a degree of volatility that we consider normal and another that we consider excessive. We have accumulated enough reserves in the last few years to deal with that excessive volatility."  \par \par \uc2 The central banker added, "The foreign investors that are buying are aware that the interest rate differential is very wide, and they should have some form of hedging."  \par \par \uc2 He also suggested that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little impetus to raise its key reference rate from the current 3.0% level, given expectations that prices will remain at current levels.  \par \par \uc2 "It doesn't make much sense to increase interest rates if we have inflation rates that are below the floor and that should remain like this in the immediate future," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to keep consumer inflation within a one percentage point range - with 2.5% serving as the midpoint - into the indefinite future.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt said Peru's consumer price index could end the year lower than the 1.8% forecast set recently by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and come in closer to the lower end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range.  \par \par \uc2 He added that inflation in 2006 should also trend in the lower range of the band, ending the year at its 2.5% mid-range level.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt noted that over the last four years inflation in Peru has averaged around 2.5%, the lowest levels since the 1930s.  \par \par \uc2 The consumer price index for Lima rose by 1.22% over the 12-month period to August.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt also said efforts to reduce the high levels of the use of the dollar in the economy are taking place on various levels, including promoting efforts to place prices of goods in stores in soles as well as in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expressed concern that borrowers who have obligations in dollars while receiving income in soles could be caught out by a shift in financial conditions.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt also said that any decision by Peru to drop its long-running series of loan programs with the International Monetary Fund will be up to the incoming government. Peruvian officials have expressed the desire for the country to amicably withdraw from its IMF loan programs. In June last year Peru received a 26-month standby loan worth some $422.8 million, but has signaled its intentions to not draw on any of the funds.  \par \par \uc2 Dancourt, an economist, took over as acting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president following the resignation of Javier Silva Ruete in late 2004, who left to work for the IMF.  \par \par \uc2 -By Eduardo Kaplan and Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com [ 28-09-05 1640GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050928e19s000ye\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
