\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Robust Economy Faces Challenges In 2005   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2005\par 19:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Red-hot metal prices, strong textile exports and development of the fledgling energy sector boosted Peru's economy last year.   \par \par \uc2 But questions remain about how strongly that momentum will carry over to 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Protesters may continue to plague the important mining sector, while the approach of the 2006 general elections could add some heat to Peru's volatile politics. Some economists also worry that slower global growth might weaken the Andean nation's vitality.   \par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product is expected to have grown by about 4.5% last year, a figure that analysts say is solid but not as good as it should be given high raw material prices and low interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 "Last year was a regular year for growth, since with 4.5% growth we just have the level of job creation needed to meet the number of people entering the labor force. We need growth of seven or eight or even nine percent," said Samuel Gleiser, a businessman and president of the national chamber of commerce, Perucamaras.   \par \par \uc2 The government forecasts GDP will again grow 4.5% this year, while many private sector economists expect output to be at least 4.0%.   \par \par \uc2 "It (2005) will be a good year, taking into account what the economy can give. To ask for greater growth, for more than 4.0% or 5.0%, is asking too much in terms in what it can grow without overheating," said Banco BBVA Continental chief economist David Tuesta.   \par \par \uc2 Banco de Credito forecasts output of 4.5% in 2005, "which would complete seven consecutive years of growth, something that hasn't happened since 1977."   \par \par \uc2 Both Fitch Ratings and Standard & Poor's lifted Peru's credit rating last year. Nonetheless, Peru is still considered a speculative play for investors, as credit rating agencies keep a close eye on the country's high debt levels and the 2006 elections.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       Resource Sector Helps Boost Growth \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the resource sector should continue to lead growth, alongside the awarding of concessions for some highway projects.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The giant Camisea natural gas project came on stream last year, and officials now point to the second phase, known as Camisea Two, aimed at producing liquefied natural gas for export.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru LNG Co., owned by Hunt Oil Peru and South Korea's SK Corp, (003600.SE) expects to start construction of a $1.6 billion LNG plant in July, although there are still lingering disputes between two regions over ownership of the land where the plant is to be built.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fishing sector officials forecast another good year for the anchovy haul, which reached more than 8.0 million metric tons last year. Peru is the world's largest producer of fishmeal from anchovy, used mainly as animal feed.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the mining sector, companies last year opposed the imposition of a new royalty on mining company sales, while various community groups led protests, citing environmental concerns. Protesters stopped exploration at Minera Yanacocha SLR's Cerro Quilish gold deposit, run by Newmont Mining Corp. (NEM), and at Buenaventura SAA's (BVN) La Zanja gold deposit.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonetheless, the Energy and Mines Ministry predicts that the mining sector will expand between 3% and 4% in 2005, as several large-scale projects remain on track.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among them, Barrick Gold Corp. (ABX) plans to make the first pour at the $340 million Alto Chicama gold project in the third quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) expects to start construction of a new concentrator early this year as part of a planned $850 million expansion.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swiss-based Xstrata PLC (XTA.LN) plans to start exploring early this year after winning the concession for the Las Bambas copper deposit in 2004, while work to modernize Southern Peru Copper Corp.'s (PCU) facilities will also continue.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             Free Trade Deal Hoped For \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The robust mining sector has sharply boosted Peru's exports, which may have reached about $12 billion in 2004 and which are expected to grow by at least 10% this year, Trade and Tourism Minister Alfredo Ferrero said recently.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru, alongside Ecuador and Colombia, will also enter what they hope will be the final phase of talks for a free trade deal with the United States. The government wants that pact to, among other things, extend the special tariff status with the U.S. that helped boost textile and garment exports by 33% in the first eleven months of 2004 compared to a year earlier.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lifting of some international quotas on textile and garment quotas from the start of the year could, however, put a crimp in the Peruvian industry as it faces greater competition.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a sense of gloom and doom until we know the effect of the disappearance of quotas for those products," Perucamaras' Gleiser said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Deficit To Remain Under Control \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong trade accounts have helped boost the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and analysts expect the sol to remain stable in 2005 owing to continued trade surpluses and low inflation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol appreciated by more than 5% against the U.S. dollar in 2004, prompting the Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy a record $2.34 billion, aiming to ease the gains. That in turn helped boost net international reserves to a record high last year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru is also expected to proceed with plans to prepay some of the $8.18 billion it owes to the Paris Club, issuing debt to ease a scheduled hump in payments over the next few years.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists also expect inflationary pressures to remain under control after supply shocks tied to a drought in Peru and higher energy costs lifted the 2004 consumer price index by 3.48%, just below the higher edge of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the government forecasts that 2005's fiscal shortfall will narrow to 1.0% of GDP after reaching an expected 1.3% or 1.4% of GDP for 2004.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some economists say efforts to reduce the fiscal deficit may weaken if the government increases spending before the elections in an effort to boost its approval ratings. They also point to a perceived lack of political will to eliminate tax exemptions.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts, moreover, will be watching closely as various politicians position themselves for a run for office in 2006. Peru's constitution prohibits Peru's president, the deeply unpopular Alejandro Toledo, from seeking a second term.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A series of accusations tied to alleged acts of corruption on the part of several of Toledo's family members has the potential to upset the political order in the new year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A group of disgruntled ex-soldiers who took over a police station in a bloody attack in a remote Andean mountain town as the year began also shook the nation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What took place gives the impression that 2005 will be highly volatile year in terms of political and social protests," political analyst Santiago Pedraglio wrote in newspaper Peru21.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;511-221-7050;peru@dowjones.com [ 05-01-05 1836GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050105e115000n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
