\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Credicorp sees sol at 3.30/dollar in 2004.   \b0\par\par\uc2 241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 22:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, Peru, Nov 9 (Reuters) - Peru's largest financial holding, Credicorp. (BAP.LM) (BAP.N), expects the sol to finish the year at around 3.30 to 3.31 to the U.S. dollar, slowing its recent appreciation, an executive said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 The sol, which closed at 3.306/3.308 to the dollar on Tuesday, strengthened 4.3 percent between January and October due to strong economic growth, rising investor confidence and an export boom.   \par \par \uc2 "We trust there won't be significant changes; we see exchange rate stability until the end of the year ... at around 3.30 to 3.31 soles to the dollar," Raimundo Morales, Credicorp's chief operating officer, told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2 Peru's Economy Minister Pedro Pablo Kuczynski last month warned an exchange rate above 3.30 soles to the dollar would be dangerous, putting the country out of sync with the world economy and making its exports too expensive.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent $1.813 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so far this year in a bid to prevent the sol from strengthening too fast.   \par \par \uc2 From this month, the bank now requires commercial banks to place 30 percent of customers' dollar deposits in its vaults, up from 20 percent in October, to help cool the sol's rise.   \par \par \uc2 The sol averaged 3.50 to the dollar in 2003.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041109e0b900ete\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
